.TH JPACKER 8 "June 2026" "jpacker @VERSION@" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
jpacker \- A robust Pacman wrapper and AUR helper with source-based build management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jpacker
.I <operation>
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ targets... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jpacker
is a C++20 pacman wrapper and AUR helper.
It extends pacman's functionality by handling AUR packages, providing one-off source builds for official packages, and managing persistent per-package source build preferences (similar to Gentoo's package.env).

Operations handled entirely by pacman pass through pacman-compatible options that jpacker does not consume.
When a sync target is routed to AUR or a source build, pacman options that cannot retain their meaning in the separated source-build lifecycle are rejected before the transaction starts instead of being ignored.
Pacman options that take values must follow the operation and may use either \fB--option value\fR or \fB--option=value\fR form. A missing value for an option tracked by jpacker stops before pacman execution.
The token after a value-taking pacman option is treated as its value even when it has the same spelling as a jpacker option. The first \fB--\fR not consumed as an option value is preserved as pacman's end-of-options marker; later tokens are opaque operands and are not interpreted as jpacker options.
Package source selectors are recognized only in normal global-option positions. A selector that appears as a pacman option value or after \fB--\fR remains part of pacman's ordered argument list.
Unsupported options for jpacker-specific operations also stop instead of being forwarded to pacman.

.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.B \-S, \-Syu, \-Syy
Install packages or update the system. If a target is found in the official repositories, pacman is used. If not, it is searched in the AUR.
If a package has a source-build preference (via \fBadd-src\fR), it will be built from source even if it exists in the official repositories.
For a plain \fB-S\fR install, \fB--aur\fR treats every root target as an AUR package without repository fallback, while \fB--repo\fR sends the ordered targets and pacman options to one official binary repository transaction without AUR or source-build fallback.
\fBjpacker -Syu\fR is treated as a pacman-compatible system upgrade and does not scan all registered source-build preferences. Use \fBjpacker upgrade\fR when registered source-build settings should be checked after the system upgrade and rebuilt when needed.
\fBjpacker -Sc\fR is passed through to \fBsudo pacman -Sc\fR and cleans pacman caches only. Use \fBjpacker clean\fR when you also want to remove jpacker build/cache files.
The jpacker cache directory is \fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jpacker\fR when \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is set, otherwise \fI$HOME/.cache/jpacker\fR.
\fBjpacker clean\fR shows the resolved jpacker cache directory at runtime and removes cached build files after confirmation. \fIjpacker.log\fR is excluded.
.TP
.B \-Ss \fI<query>\fR
Search for packages in both the official repositories and the AUR.
AUR search results may show status tags such as \fB[installed]\fR, \fB[out-of-date]\fR, and \fB[orphaned]\fR when that metadata is available.
In Auto mode, when refresh is requested with \fB-y\fR or \fB--refresh\fR, the official repository search uses \fBsudo pacman\fR before the usual AUR search.
\fB--aur\fR searches only AUR and does not run pacman; combining it with refresh is rejected. \fB--repo\fR searches only official repositories and retains plain-pacman or refresh-triggered sudo routing.
.TP
.B \-Si \fI<package>\fR
Show package information. Official repository packages use pacman output; AUR packages use AUR metadata and include installed, orphaned, and out-of-date state.
In Auto mode, when refresh is requested, every target must use a repository-qualified form such as \fBcore/filesystem\fR. An unqualified target stops before pacman, sudo, or an AUR query so an official database refresh cannot run before a possible AUR fallback is known. Without refresh, the existing repository-first AUR fallback remains available.
\fB--aur\fR shows only AUR metadata and does not run pacman; repository-qualified targets and refresh are rejected. \fB--repo\fR shows only official repository information, never falls back to AUR, and allows refresh with qualified or unqualified targets through the existing sudo routing.
.TP
.B \-F, \-Fl \fI<query>\fR
Search or list the pacman file database without sudo. Forms that request a file database refresh, including \fB-Fy\fR, \fB-Fyy\fR, \fB-F -y\fR, and \fB-F --refresh\fR, use \fBsudo pacman\fR.
.TP
.B \-R, \-Rs, \-Rns \fI<target>\fR
Remove packages. This is a direct wrapper around \fBpacman -R\fR.
.TP
.B build \fI<package> [VAR=VALUE...]\fR
Perform a one-off build from source for a package (either from Official Repos or AUR).
Environment variables (like CFLAGS) can be passed directly.
.br
Example: \fIjpacker build coreutils CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native"\fR
.TP
.B deps [\-\-recursive] \fI<package>\fR
Inspect an AUR package's runtime, make, and check dependencies, then classify them as official repository dependencies, AUR dependencies, provided dependencies, ambiguous provider candidates, or unknown dependencies.
With \fB\-\-recursive\fR, recursively inspect AUR dependencies. Official repository, provided, ambiguous, and unknown dependencies are shown but not traversed.
If exactly one provider is found, it may be shown as a provided dependency. If multiple providers are found, jpacker reports the dependency as ambiguous and does not choose one implicitly.
Version constraints such as \fBfoo>=1.2\fR are detected and shown, but jpacker v1.x does not verify them with a complete pacman/libalpm-compatible solver.
If the target package declares AUR \fBConflicts\fR or \fBReplaces\fR metadata, it is shown separately as a metadata warning. This warning does not change the command's successful inspection status.
This command does not clone, build, install dependencies, or calculate build order.
.TP
.B plan \fI<package>\fR
Display a build order plan for an AUR package and its recursive AUR dependencies.
Official repository and repository-provided dependencies are not included in the build plan. Unknown dependencies are reported as unresolved.
After the existing plan body, jpacker displays direct official repository dependencies and uniquely resolved providers from configured official repositories in a separate \fBRepository package sizes\fR section. AUR build units and AUR providers are not assigned estimated sizes.
\fBPackage size\fR is the compressed repository package archive size, while \fBInstalled size\fR is the installed contents size. jpacker reads existing sync databases without refreshing, updating, or downloading them, and distinguishes known zero, not-found, and unavailable metadata.
Metadata unavailability does not suppress the existing plan body or by itself change \fBPlan status\fR or the command's exit status. This presentation does not change pacman pass-through, \fB-Ss\fR / \fB-Si\fR routing, dependency resolution, build/install behavior, or transactions.
If multiple providers remain for a dependency, the plan is reported as incomplete with ambiguous provider candidates; jpacker does not choose one implicitly.
Unverified version constraints are reported as unresolved reasons and are not treated as satisfied.
If the requested AUR package has a package name different from its PackageBase, the plan shows it as a split package install target and reports the plan as incomplete because install target selection is not implemented.
Conflicts/replaces metadata for every AUR package in the recursive plan is shown with its package name. A plan containing this metadata is incomplete because jpacker v1.x does not resolve it automatically.
This command does not clone, build, or install dependencies.
.TP
.B \-G \fI<package>\fR
Clone exactly one requested AUR package's complete PackageBase repository into \fI./<PackageBase>\fR below the current directory.
This is an AUR-only operation: it does not probe official repositories, consult source-build preferences, fall back to a repository package, or retrieve dependency repositories. A split package is exported under its PackageBase name.
If any directory, repository, file, symlink, dangling symlink, or other entry already exists at the destination, the command fails without fetching, updating, overwriting, or removing it. \fB--noconfirm\fR and all other global or pacman options are unsupported and cannot bypass this guard.
jpacker anchors the command-start current directory and invocation-owned temporary directory with directory descriptors and device/inode identity. It verifies the expected AUR remote, \fI.git\fR, and a regular non-symlink \fIPKGBUILD\fR, then publishes with descriptor-relative no-replace rename. It does not use or modify the jpacker build cache and does not run makepkg, pacman, build, or install operations.
.TP
.B \-Gp \fI<package>\fR
Print only the root \fIPKGBUILD\fR bytes from exactly one requested AUR package's PackageBase repository to standard output.
The PackageBase is resolved by exact AUR package metadata, including for split packages. The repository is cloned into a descriptor-anchored secure temporary directory; the expected remote and regular non-symlink \fIPKGBUILD\fR are validated. No persistent checkout remains after normal success or ordinary failure. PKGBUILD is opened relative to the checkout descriptor without following symlinks.
Successful stdout contains only PKGBUILD content. Diagnostics, command display, and PackageBase mapping are written to stderr; failures before stdout emission leave stdout empty. If a temporary-path identity mismatch is detected, jpacker fails closed rather than deleting that replacement and may leave temporary artifacts for manual inspection. The command does not evaluate PKGBUILD, generate or print \fI.install\fR / \fI.SRCINFO\fR, retrieve dependencies, use the jpacker cache, or run makepkg, pacman, build, or install operations. Global and pacman options are unsupported.
.TP
.B fetch \fI<package>\fR
Safely retrieve the AUR build repositories needed to inspect an AUR package and its recursive AUR dependencies before any build/install stage.
Fetch targets are resolved by PackageBase. Official repository and provided dependencies are not cloned. Unknown dependencies are reported as unresolved.
Split package install target selection is not required for fetch itself; if the requested package resolves to a PackageBase and the fetch plan has no unresolved, ambiguous, or cyclic issue, fetch may still clone or fetch that PackageBase.
Conflicts/replaces metadata is shown as a warning but does not prevent fetch, because fetch is limited to read-only repository retrieval.
Missing AUR repositories are cloned. Existing cloned repositories run only \fBgit fetch origin\fR.
This command does not update the working tree and does not run \fBgit pull\fR, merge, reset, build, or install operations.
Future behavior that advances a working tree is not implemented by \fBfetch\fR and should be considered in a separate issue as a command or option such as \fBsync\fR, \fBupdate\fR, or \fBfetch --update\fR.
.TP
.B upgrade
Perform a system upgrade and configured rebuilds. This first runs \fBsudo pacman -Syu\fR, then checks packages with source-build preferences and rebuilds them from source only when the version in the existing \fI.SRCINFO\fR is newer than the installed package.
Use this command, not \fBjpacker -Syu\fR, when registered source-build settings in \fI/etc/jpacker/package.build/\fR should be checked automatically after a system upgrade.
During existing cache repository updates, jpacker can show \fBHEAD..origin/<branch>\fR before resetting the working tree. Initial clones have no previous checkout to compare against, so no update diff prompt is shown.
Before build/install, jpacker prompts to review \fIPKGBUILD\fR and reports top-level \fI*.install\fR files when present. This does not evaluate \fIPKGBUILD\fR to resolve \fBinstall=\fR; it only highlights install scripts already present in the working tree.
\fBjpacker upgrade\fR uses the existing \fI.SRCINFO\fR in the working tree for pre-review update checks. If \fI.SRCINFO\fR is missing or incomplete, jpacker does not run \fBmakepkg --printsrcinfo\fR before review; interactive runs ask whether to continue, while \fB--noconfirm\fR or non-interactive runs skip the package.
.TP
.B upgrade-aur
Update installed AUR packages only. This target-less operation inventories foreign packages in the local package database and considers only packages that still resolve to an exact AUR package. Official repository packages and foreign packages absent from AUR are not update targets. A source-build preference is not required to select an installed AUR package; a matching preference may still supply its existing build environment.
This operation does not run \fBpacman -Syu\fR and does not update the system or packages selected only by source-build preferences. Run \fBjpacker -Syu\fR or \fBjpacker upgrade\fR separately for those scopes. Combined updates use the separate \fBjpacker upgrade-all\fR contract and do not expand the scope of \fBupgrade-aur\fR.
jpacker validates the complete AUR update plan before source mutation, then executes prepared build/install work items sequentially and stops at the first failure. A failure does not roll back packages already updated; remaining work is reported as not attempted, partial completion returns a non-zero status, and package, reason, and retained/cleanup workspace status are shown. A cleanup failure after a successful package transaction also returns non-zero even though that package is already updated.
When no installed AUR package needs an update, the command succeeds without starting git, makepkg, pacman, or sudo. Supported options are \fB--noedit\fR, \fB--nodiff\fR, \fB--noconfirm\fR, \fB--rebuild\fR, and \fB--cleanbuild\fR. Package targets, \fB--needed\fR, \fB--rmdeps\fR, \fB--aur\fR, and \fB--repo\fR are unsupported and stop before update execution.
.TP
.B upgrade-all
Update the system, registered source packages, and remaining installed AUR packages in one target-less aggregate operation. It runs \fBsudo pacman -Syu\fR, processes registered source-build preferences, obtains a fresh foreign package inventory, and then updates the filtered AUR targets in that order. It does not change the separate contracts of \fBupgrade\fR, \fBupgrade-aur\fR, \fB-Syu\fR, or \fB-Qua\fR.
Explicit source preferences take precedence over automatically detected AUR targets. An AUR target is excluded when its package name or PackageBase is already handled by an explicit source package. A transitive AUR build unit whose PackageBase is provided by an explicit source preference is treated as externally satisfied instead of being built and installed twice.
This is not a single atomic transaction. Execution stops on the first phase failure or cleanup failure, does not automatically roll back packages already updated, and reports later phases or work items as not attempted. Partial completion and cleanup failure return non-zero, even when an earlier package transaction succeeded.
Supported options are \fB--noedit\fR, \fB--nodiff\fR, \fB--noconfirm\fR, \fB--rebuild\fR, and \fB--cleanbuild\fR. Package targets, opaque operands, \fB--needed\fR, \fB--rmdeps\fR, \fB--aur\fR, and \fB--repo\fR are unsupported and stop before default log/cache initialization, source preference preparation, the system upgrade, foreign inventory, or an AUR query.
.TP
.B clean
Clean package/build caches. This first runs \fBsudo pacman -Sc\fR to clean the official package cache, and then prompts to remove cached build files from the resolved jpacker cache directory. The directory is \fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jpacker\fR when \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is set, otherwise \fI$HOME/.cache/jpacker\fR. \fIjpacker.log\fR is preserved.
.TP
.B add-src \fI<package> [VAR=VALUE...]\fR
Enable a source-build preference for a package. This creates a preference file in \fI/etc/jpacker/package.build/\fR.
You can optionally append build environment variables to the preference.
.TP
.B del-src \fI<package>\fR
Remove a source-build preference. Removes the preference file.
.TP
.B revert \fI<package>\fR
Remove a source-build preference AND immediately reinstall the official binary version (via \fBpacman -S\fR) if the package exists in the official repositories. Use this to restore a package to its standard state.
.TP
.B edit-src \fI<package>\fR
Open the source-build preference file for the specified package using the defined \fBEDITOR\fR.
.TP
.B list-src
List source-build preferences along with their custom environment variables.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-noedit
Skip the interactive review/edit of \fIPKGBUILD\fR and top-level \fI*.install\fR files before building.
.TP
.BR \-\-nodiff
Skip the interactive git diff check during existing cache repository updates.
.TP
.BR \-\-noconfirm
Pass \fB--noconfirm\fR to pacman and makepkg execution. jpacker treats this as a request to avoid interactive blocking, not as "yes to everything". This does not bypass jpacker's ambiguous provider, unresolved dependency, cyclic build plan, split package install target, or conflicts/replaces metadata checks.
.TP
.BR \-\-needed
Treat this as a pacman-compatible option, not a jpacker global option. Pacman-only routes retain it in the ordered pacman arguments. For a supported \fB-S\fR target routed to AUR or an official source-build preference, pass it once to the final \fBsudo pacman -U\fR transaction. The option itself does not skip validation, planning, safety guards, review, clone/fetch, or the path to makepkg execution. jpacker does not add an installed-version or local-artifact build-skip heuristic; each PackageBase is built into a fresh \fIPKGDEST\fR. AUR and official source-build preference routes use the same meaning. Target-less pacman-compatible \fB-Syu --needed\fR is passed through; a source target in an existing supported form receives the same install-only policy, while jpacker's custom \fBupgrade --needed\fR, \fBupgrade-aur --needed\fR, and \fBupgrade-all --needed\fR remain unsupported.
.TP
.BR \-\-rebuild
Pass \fB-f\fR to build-only makepkg execution. When omitted, jpacker may ask before rebuilding an existing local package artifact; the default answer is no.
.TP
.BR \-\-cleanbuild
Pass \fB-C\fR to build-only makepkg execution so the existing \fIsrcdir\fR is not reused. When omitted, jpacker may ask before cleaning an existing local \fIsrc/\fR directory; the default answer is no. If a local package artifact exists in the same package directory, cleanbuild also adds \fB-f\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-rmdeps
Recognize this as a jpacker option, but reject an invocation that selects the separated AUR/source-build route before artifact workspace creation, makepkg, an installed metadata query, or sudo. \fBupgrade-aur\fR and \fBupgrade-all\fR reject it before checking whether updates are available. It is not translated to \fBmakepkg -r\fR, and jpacker does not run its own pacman removal or orphan cleanup. This option has no effect on and is not passed to pacman-only installs.
.TP
.BR \-\-aur
Limit plain \fB-S\fR installs, \fB-Ss\fR searches, and \fB-Si\fR information queries to AUR, with no official repository fallback. Official packages with the same name still use AUR, and source-build preferences and the official source-build route are ignored. Repository-qualified root targets are invalid. AUR build-plan dependencies retain their existing handling.
.TP
.BR \-\-repo
Limit plain \fB-S\fR installs, \fB-Ss\fR searches, and \fB-Si\fR information queries to official binary repositories, with no AUR or source-build fallback. A source-build preference is overridden for this invocation only; its preference file is not modified or removed. Repository-qualified targets are accepted.
.PP
Repeating the same source selector is allowed. Combining \fB--aur\fR and \fB--repo\fR fails before any external command or AUR query, and \fB--noconfirm\fR does not bypass this conflict or the selected source. Source selectors are not supported for upgrade/refresh/clean sync operations, \fB-Qua\fR, or jpacker-specific operations such as \fBupgrade\fR, \fBupgrade-aur\fR, \fBupgrade-all\fR, \fBbuild\fR, \fBfetch\fR, \fBdeps\fR, \fBplan\fR, \fB\-G\fR, and \fB\-Gp\fR. The exception is repository-only \fB-Ss\fR / \fB-Si\fR with refresh, which uses \fBsudo pacman\fR.
.PP
AUR/source builds create an invocation-owned fresh \fIPKGDEST\fR for each PackageBase, determine the expected artifact with \fBmakepkg --packagelist\fR, and then run build-only \fBmakepkg -sc\fR. makepkg builds source package artifacts, jpacker owns the artifact workspace, validation, and install policy, and \fBpacman -U\fR owns the validated-artifact install transaction. Semantic \fB--needed\fR is passed once only to that \fBpacman -U\fR transaction. \fB--noconfirm\fR is passed to both build-only makepkg and \fBpacman -U\fR, while \fB--rebuild\fR and \fB--cleanbuild\fR add \fB-f\fR and \fB-C\fR to makepkg. An inherited or source-environment \fIPKGDEST\fR definition, including an empty value, is rejected during all-target preflight before any source unit starts. \fB--rmdeps\fR is likewise rejected instead of becoming \fBmakepkg -r\fR. Other pacman transaction options are not translated to makepkg; if an AUR/source-build target is present, unsupported options stop the invocation before pacman or makepkg execution. An option value named \fB--needed\fR, an operand after \fB--\fR, and \fB--needed=true\fR do not enable the source install policy. AUR-only \fB-Ss\fR / \fB-Si\fR reject \fB--needed\fR before an AUR query, while repository-only forms delegate it to pacman.
.PP
The current route accepts exactly one artifact per PackageBase. Split-package install-target selection, sibling or debug packages, and multiple outputs are unsupported and fail closed. A failure in post-build validation, a metadata query, or installation retains the artifact workspace for diagnostics, but a later invocation does not reuse it automatically. If workspace cleanup fails after a successful \fBpacman -U\fR transaction, the package is already installed; do not blindly retry the installation.
.PP
jpacker v1.x does not compare AUR conflicts/replaces metadata with installed or official repository packages, use replacements to select install targets, or automatically remove or replace packages. Any AUR build/install plan containing this metadata stops before clone, fetch, build, makepkg, or a pacman transaction.
.TP
.BR \-h , \ \-\-help
Display the help message and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The main configuration file is located at \fI/etc/jpacker/jpacker.conf\fR.
It supports the following keys:
.TP
.B NOEDIT
Set to \fBtrue\fR to disable \fIPKGBUILD\fR / \fI*.install\fR review prompts globally.
.TP
.B NODIFF
Set to \fBtrue\fR to disable git diff checks during existing cache repository updates globally.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Specify the preferred text editor. The priority is the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable, this configuration value, then \fBnano\fR.
Editor command tokens are restricted to simple command words and simple options for safety. For example, \fBnano\fR, \fBvim\fR, and \fBcode --wait\fR are supported. Complex shell syntax such as shell expansion, nested quoting, pipes, redirects, or command chains is intentionally unsupported.
.TP
.B LOGFILE
Specify the path to the log file. Supports tilde expansion (e.g., \fB~/logs/jpacker.log\fR).
Default is \fI~/.cache/jpacker/jpacker.log\fR.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/jpacker/jpacker.conf
Global configuration file. Controls editor settings, log locations, and review prompts.
A normal \fBmake uninstall\fR preserves this file. Remove it manually only when complete removal is explicitly intended.
.TP
.I /etc/jpacker/package.build/
Directory containing source-build preference files managed with \fBadd-src\fR.
Each file corresponds to a package name and can define environment variables (e.g., CFLAGS) that are applied during \fBmakepkg\fR execution.
A normal \fBmake uninstall\fR preserves source-build preference files in this directory, but may remove the directory itself when it is empty. Use \fBdel-src\fR or \fBrevert\fR to remove an individual preference; remove any remaining preference files manually only when complete removal is explicitly intended.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jpacker/
Default directory used for downloading and building packages when \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is not set. If \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is set, jpacker uses \fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jpacker\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jpacker/jpacker.log
Default log file location.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/jpacker
Bash completion file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/zsh/site-functions/_jpacker
zsh completion file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/jpacker.fish
fish completion file.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
The editor used for reviewing PKGBUILDs and editing configurations.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Used to determine the build directory location.

.SH EXAMPLES
.B Update the system and packages with source-build preferences:
.RS
jpacker upgrade
.RE

.B Update the system without scanning registered source-build preferences:
.RS
jpacker -Syu
.RE

.B Update installed AUR packages without running a system upgrade:
.RS
jpacker upgrade-aur
.RE

.B Update the system, registered source packages, and remaining installed AUR packages:
.RS
jpacker upgrade-all
.RE

.B Install a package from AUR without review:
.RS
jpacker -S google-chrome --noedit
.RE

.B Install a root target from AUR without repository fallback:
.RS
jpacker -S --aur google-chrome
.RE

.B Install an official binary while overriding a source-build preference for this invocation:
.RS
jpacker -S --repo fastfetch
.RE

.B Install a package without pacman/makepkg confirmation prompts:
.RS
jpacker --noconfirm -S google-chrome
.RE

.B Classify dependencies of an AUR package:
.RS
jpacker deps google-chrome
.RE

.B Recursively inspect AUR dependencies:
.RS
jpacker deps --recursive google-chrome
.RE

.B Show a build order plan for an AUR package:
.RS
jpacker plan mongodb-bin
.RE

.B Safely clone or fetch AUR build repositories:
.RS
jpacker fetch mongodb-bin
.RE

.B Export one AUR PackageBase repository into the current directory:
.RS
jpacker -G google-chrome
.RE

.B Print only an AUR PackageBase PKGBUILD to stdout:
.RS
jpacker -Gp google-chrome
.RE

.B Enable a source-build preference with custom optimizations:
.RS
jpacker add-src neofetch CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native"
.RE

.B Clean caches to free up space:
.RS
jpacker clean
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pacman (8),
.BR makepkg (8)
.PP
For the detailed operation and option pass-through policy, see the repository document \fIdocs/COMPATIBILITY.md\fR.

.SH AUTHOR
Written by seekerkrt.
